{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Instructor Implementation Toolkit","title":"Blackboard-to-ALGET Alignment","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting blackboard-to-alget alignment to the before/after constructive-alignment table (objective-to-activity-to-assessment map), reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ail606-supplement-08-07-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the before/after constructive-alignment table (objective-to-activity-to-assessment map) shows a defensible decision in AIL 606"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-08-07-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-08-07-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-08-07-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next"}],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_instructor_implementation_toolkit","ail606_supplement_blackboard_to_alget_alignment","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"08","section":"07","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/08/06"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_8_7_blackboard_to_alget_alignment_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_8_7_blackboard_to_alget_alignment_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_8_7_blackboard_to_alget_alignment_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_8_7_blackboard_to_alget_alignment_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"ail606-supplement-08-07-lo-01":["ail606_supplement_8_7_blackboard_to_alget_alignment_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_8_7_blackboard_to_alget_alignment_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_8_7_blackboard_to_alget_alignment_ai_boundary"],"ail606-supplement-08-07-lo-02":["ail606_supplement_8_7_blackboard_to_alget_alignment_calibration"],"ail606-supplement-08-07-lo-03":["ail606_supplement_8_7_blackboard_to_alget_alignment_calibration"],"ail606-supplement-08-07-lo-04":["ail606_supplement_8_7_blackboard_to_alget_alignment_annotation_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Blackboard-to-ALGET Alignment\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Blackboard To Alget Alignment, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_instructor_implementation_toolkit\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/08-07-blackboard-to-alget-alignment.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Blackboard-to-ALGET Alignment: Blackboard To Alget Alignment, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nMigrating a course from one platform to another tempts you to treat it as a transfer problem: copy the modules, repoint the links, done. But platforms differ in what they can capture, and a faithful copy of the wrong structure carries the original's misalignments into the new system intact. Moving from Blackboard to ALGET is an opportunity to ask the question the original course may have skipped: does every learning objective actually have an activity that develops it and an assessment that measures it?\n\nThis matters because ALGET's adaptive features depend on process evidence (the trace of how a learner reasoned), and a Blackboard dropbox captures only a finished file. A lift-and-shift migration hands ALGET a product with no process to read, disabling the very feature you migrated for. Alignment work is what turns a platform change into a course improvement rather than a more expensive way to deliver the same gaps.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the before/after constructive-alignment table (objective-to-activity-to-assessment map) shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA coordinator migrates a design module from Blackboard to ALGET over a weekend by exporting the course package and importing it whole. Everything appears: the Module 8 quiz, the storyboard dropbox, the gradebook columns. It looks like a clean move. Then the team maps the imported activities to the four stated objectives and finds the truth the export hid: all ten quiz items measure Objective 1 (recall of definitions), Objectives 2 through 4 have no activity at all, and the dropbox captures finished files that ALGET's adaptive support cannot read because there is no process trace.\n\nThe case shows that a successful import is not a successful migration. The package transferred faithfully; the alignment never existed to transfer. As you read, treat migration as the moment to build the objective-to-activity-to-assessment map the original course was missing, not as a chance to reproduce its structure faster.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nBlackboard-to-ALGET alignment applies constructive alignment, Biggs's principle that learning objectives, learning activities, and assessments must all target the same intended verbs, to a platform migration. In a well-aligned course, if an objective says \"analyze a defensible decision,\" some activity has students analyzing decisions and some assessment captures that analysis. Misalignment is the silent default: courses accumulate quizzes and dropboxes that measure whatever was easy to build, not what the objectives promised.\n\nThree ideas make alignment operational during a migration:\n\n- **The alignment table is the diagnosis.** A two-column table (objective, then the activity and assessment that target it) exposes empty cells. The lift-and-shift left three of four cells empty; \"we moved everything\" is an inventory, not an alignment.\n- **Match the response format to the cognitive verb.** A multiple-choice item can test recall (Objective 1) but cannot capture analysis or a documented AI-boundary decision. Forcing a higher-order objective into a recall format measures the wrong construct and adds load on a skill the item was never meant to assess.\n- **Migrate for the new platform's affordances.** ALGET reads process traces, so a storyboard dropbox should become an artifact-studio before-and-after trace that captures the decision and the revision, feeding the adaptive support that motivated the migration.\n\n| Objective verb | Blackboard activity (lifted) | Aligned ALGET activity |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Recall definitions | 10 MC quiz items | One or two MC items retained |\n| Analyze a defensible decision | none | Artifact-studio before/after trace |\n| Document a bounded AI move | none | Misconception-tagged practice on the AI boundary |\n| Decide next revision | none | Trace plus adaptive-support prompt |\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"The decision rule says build the alignment table before you migrate a single activity. Why does the table have to come first?\" answer=\"The table is the diagnosis: a two-column objective-to-activity map exposes empty cells where a higher-order objective has no activity. If you migrate activities first, you inherit whatever the old platform happened to contain (here, ten recall items and a dropbox) and reproduce its misalignment faster. Only the table tells you which cells to fill and with what format.\"></self-explain>\n\nMatch each objective verb to the ALGET activity whose response format actually fits it. A recall item cannot stand in for an analysis objective.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Recall definitions\",\"definition\":\"One or two retained multiple-choice items: a recognition format suited to recall, the only objective the lifted quiz actually served\"},{\"term\":\"Analyze a defensible decision\",\"definition\":\"Artifact-studio before-and-after trace that captures the decision and the revision ALGET adaptive support can read\"},{\"term\":\"Document a bounded AI move\",\"definition\":\"Misconception-tagged practice item that probes the AI-boundary judgment rather than recall\"},{\"term\":\"Decide next revision\",\"definition\":\"Process trace plus an adaptive-support prompt that elicits the learner next move\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWalk through one concrete alignment decision: moving a design module's assessments from Blackboard into ALGET without losing the link between learning objectives and what each activity measures.\n\n**Before (the lift-and-shift copy).** The first migration copied the Blackboard structure verbatim: a \"Module 8 Quiz\" (10 auto-graded multiple-choice items) and a \"Storyboard Dropbox\" (single upload, instructor grades later). Mapped against the four learning objectives, two problems surfaced. The 10 quiz items all measured Objective 1 (recall of definitions); Objectives 2 through 4 (analyze a defensible decision, document a bounded AI move, decide next revision) had no aligned activity at all. And the dropbox captured a finished file but no process evidence, so ALGET's adaptive support had nothing to read.\n\n1. **Build the alignment table first, not the activities.** List each objective in one column and the activity that measures it in the next. The lift-and-shift left three cells empty. Alignment is the diagnosis; \"we moved everything\" is not.\n2. **Map each ALGET feature to the objective it serves.** The storyboard upload becomes an artifact-studio before/after trace (serves Objective 2 and 4, because it captures the decision and the revision). One quiz item is rewritten as a misconception-tagged practice item that probes Objective 3 (the AI-boundary decision). By **[cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load)**, matching the response format to the objective keeps assessment load on the target skill rather than on recall it was not meant to test.\n3. **Confirm coverage before going live.** Re-check the table: every objective now has at least one aligned ALGET activity, and no activity is orphaned from an objective.\n\n**After (the course as an aligned migration).** The alignment table moved from 1 of 4 objectives covered to 4 of 4, with the dropbox replaced by a trace that ALGET's adaptive support can actually read. The change is auditable: a reviewer can compare the lift-and-shift inventory to the objective-to-activity table and see which empty cells were filled and how.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBuild the alignment table before you build or migrate a single activity. The table is the diagnosis; activities chosen without it inherit whatever the old platform happened to contain. Migrate toward the new platform's strengths, not toward the old platform's habits.\n\nRun any migration plan through three checks:\n\n1. **Coverage check:** Does every objective have at least one activity that develops it and one assessment that measures it, with no empty cells? A lifted course usually fails this on its higher-order objectives.\n2. **Verb-format match check:** Does each assessment's response format fit the objective's cognitive verb? A recall item cannot stand in for an analysis objective, no matter how cleanly it imported.\n3. **Affordance check:** Does the migrated activity use what the new platform can do that the old one could not? If a dropbox stays a dropbox, ALGET's process-reading features have nothing to read, and the migration bought nothing.\n\nA plan that fails these moved the course without improving it, and often disabled the features that justified moving.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A coordinator replaces the Blackboard dropbox with an artifact-studio block but tells students only to upload their finished storyboard there. Why does this fail the affordance check?\" explanation=\"Adding the new platform's feature is not the same as using it. An artifact-studio that students treat as a second dropbox elicits no process trace, so ALGET's adaptive support receives the same dead-end product. The affordance has to be wired to a task that produces what it can read.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Treated as a second dropbox, it elicits no process trace, so the process-reading feature has nothing to read.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Artifact-studio blocks cannot accept storyboards at all.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The dropbox was already aligned, so no change was needed.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Uploading a finished file is the strongest evidence of analysis.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common misread is that a complete import equals a complete migration. The export tool copies whatever exists, including the gaps; a flawless transfer of a misaligned course is a flawless transfer of misalignment. Fidelity to the source is not a virtue when the source was never aligned.\n\nA second misread is that adding the new platform's features is the same as using them. Replacing a dropbox with an artifact-studio block only helps if the activity actually elicits a process trace; an artifact-studio that students treat as a second dropbox feeds ALGET the same dead-end product. The affordance has to be wired to a task that produces what it can read.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nRe-align the lifted module step by step. Start with the table, not the activities: list the four objectives down one column. The import filled only the recall row; three rows are empty, which is the real finding the clean import concealed.\n\nNow fill the cells by matching format to verb. Objective 2 (analyze a defensible decision) and Objective 4 (decide next revision) both need captured reasoning, so the storyboard dropbox becomes an artifact-studio before-and-after trace that records the decision and the revision. Objective 3 (document a bounded AI move) needs a probe of judgment, so one quiz item is rewritten as a misconception-tagged practice item on the AI boundary. Objective 1 keeps a couple of the recall items, which were never the problem.\n\nFinally, re-check the table for coverage and orphans: every objective now maps to an aligned activity, and no activity floats unattached to an objective. The walkthrough is complete when the migration has moved from one of four objectives covered to four of four, and the dropbox has become a trace ALGET's adaptive support can actually read, which is the whole reason the course moved platforms.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader of this section instinctively builds the alignment table when handed any course, looking first for empty cells where an objective has no activity or assessment. Constructive alignment is the lens; the empty cell is the defect it reveals.\n\nRead any migrated or inherited course against these four questions:\n\n1. Does every objective map to an activity that develops it and an assessment that measures it?\n2. Does each assessment's format match the cognitive verb of its objective, or does a recall item stand in for higher-order work?\n3. Does the migration exploit the new platform's affordances, or merely reproduce the old structure?\n4. Is any activity orphaned, present in the course but attached to no objective?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong alignment artifact shows the before table (objectives versus lifted activities, with empty cells visible) and the after table (every cell filled with a format-matched activity), plus a note on which dropbox became a process-capturing trace. A reviewer should be able to see coverage move from one of four objectives to four of four and to see why each new activity fits its objective's verb.\n\nA strong rationale sounds like this: \"I converted the storyboard dropbox into an artifact-studio trace because Objectives 2 and 4 require captured reasoning that a finished-file upload cannot show, and ALGET's adaptive support needs a process trace to read. I rejected the suggestion to keep all ten quiz items, because nine of them measured recall that only Objective 1 called for and left the higher-order objectives unassessed.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same alignment work to a migration into a platform that, unlike ALGET, cannot capture process traces and supports only quizzes and file uploads. Constructive alignment is unchanged, but the affordance constraint inverts: you cannot rely on a process-reading feature to assess the analysis objectives. The faithful transfer is to meet the higher-order objectives with what the platform can do, perhaps a structured upload whose template forces a documented before-and-after rationale, and to accept heavier instructor reading where automated reading is impossible. Copying the ALGET artifact-studio expectation into a platform that cannot read traces would leave the analysis objectives nominally covered but actually unassessable.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the before/after constructive-alignment table (objective-to-activity-to-assessment map) packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/08-07-blackboard-to-alget-alignment-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"before/after constructive-alignment table (objective-to-activity-to-assessment map)\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"08.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the before/after constructive-alignment table (objective-to-activity-to-assessment map).\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the before/after constructive-alignment table (objective-to-activity-to-assessment map) for AIL 606 08.07. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_blackboard_to_alget_alignment\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links Blackboard-to-ALGET Alignment, evidence source, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates design learning from simple completion.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_instructor_implementation_toolkit\", \"ail606_supplement_blackboard_to_alget_alignment\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_7_blackboard_to_alget_alignment_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_blackboard_to_alget_alignment","stem":"A team copies its Blackboard module structure verbatim into ALGET and calls the migration 'aligned.' What is the strongest correction?","options":["It is aligned; everything was moved successfully.","Copying is not aligning; build an objective-to-activity table and check each objective has a matching ALGET activity.","Alignment just means the links open in ALGET.","Alignment means the modules appear in the same order."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Alignment is the mapping of objectives to activities, not the reproduction of the old structure.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_8_7_copy_is_alignment","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_blackboard_to_alget_alignment"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-08-07-lo-01"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_7_blackboard_to_alget_alignment_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"The migrated quiz has 10 recall items, all measuring Objective 1 (define terms). Objectives 2 to 4 ask students to analyze, document, and decide. What does the alignment table reveal?","options":["All four objectives are covered because there are ten items.","Three objectives have no aligned activity; recall items cannot measure a design decision.","The quiz is fine because recall is the foundation.","Objectives 2 to 4 are optional anyway."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Recall items cannot show analysis, documentation, or decision; those objective cells are empty.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_8_7_recall_covers_all","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-08-07-lo-01","ail606-supplement-08-07-lo-04"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_7_blackboard_to_alget_alignment_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"An instructor uses AI to suggest which ALGET feature serves each objective. What is the strongest boundary?","options":["Adopt the AI mapping without checking it.","Use the AI suggestions, then verify each objective truly maps to a feature that can show that skill, leaving no orphaned activity.","Avoid AI and copy the Blackboard structure as-is.","Ask AI only to rename the activities."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI can propose mappings, but the instructor must confirm each objective is covered and no activity is orphaned.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_8_7_dropbox_loses_process","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-08-07-lo-01"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_7_blackboard_to_alget_alignment_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"Why replace the single-file storyboard dropbox with an artifact-studio before/after trace during migration?","options":["The trace makes the gradebook simpler.","The dropbox captures only a finished file, so ALGET's adaptive support has no process evidence to act on.","Traces are required by Blackboard.","A trace looks more modern than a dropbox."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Adaptive support needs process evidence; a single-file upload records completion but no decision trail.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_8_7_dropbox_loses_process","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-08-07-lo-02","ail606-supplement-08-07-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_7_copy_is_alignment","pattern":"lift_and_shift_treated_as_alignment","description":"Believing copying a Blackboard course structure verbatim into ALGET is alignment","trigger":"migration reproduces the old structure without mapping activities to objectives","feedback":"Build an objective-to-activity table first; moving everything is not the same as aligning anything.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_7_recall_covers_all","pattern":"recall_items_cover_higher_objectives","description":"Assuming recall multiple-choice items measure analysis, documentation, and decision objectives","trigger":"all assessment items test definitions while higher objectives have no aligned activity","feedback":"Match each objective to a response format that can show it; recall items cannot measure a design decision.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_7_dropbox_loses_process","pattern":"file_upload_without_process_evidence","description":"Keeping a single-file dropbox that captures no process for adaptive support to read","trigger":"migrated activity stores a finished file but no before/after trace","feedback":"Replace the dropbox with an artifact trace so ALGET's adaptive support has process evidence to act on.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"fb13e153682c30b86dff321df371dc62c5aa9865b8ba0380349369937c603a8c","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}